00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:39.170
So you might ask
yourself then, why

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:41.990
would I want to use the cloud?

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:45.320
Why not just deploy this
on a local environment

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:48.590
in on premise server?

00:11:48.590 --> 00:11:50.950
So for those who are
uninitiated with the cloud,

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:52.580
I'll take it slow.

00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:55.580
There are some major
benefits to using the Cloud.

00:11:55.580 --> 00:12:00.620
One of the most cited use cases
is that you get paid as you go,

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:04.020
or rather you only
pay for what you need.

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:06.390
You also lower your
total cost of ownership,

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:09.180
so you're not paying
for massive overheads.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:12.400
You take a lot of the
guestimating out of your work.

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:17.060
So you can plan
scalable infrastructure.

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:19.550
You can focus more on
innovation as opposed

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:23.630
to managing and maintaining
expensive infrastructure.

00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:25.400
And you can utilize
the global networks

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:27.650
into ever expanding global
networks of different Cloud

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:30.530
providers to make sure
your teams across the globe

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:31.760
can interact with each other.

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:34.520
This is very important
for large data science

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:37.400
teams that are spread out
across many different sites

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:38.210
across the globe.

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:44.370
A small bit deeper into
the pay as you go model.

00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:46.080
What does it mean,
when you trade

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:49.710
in the expensive and rigid
on premise hardware for more

00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:52.170
highly available Cloud
infrastructure, only paying

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:54.030
for what you use?

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:56.180
No more or less.

00:12:56.180 --> 00:13:00.500
And so many providers
provide this model,

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:03.230
and this means there's
a very low or sometimes

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:04.910
even no upfront cost.

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:06.560
So with some service
providers, you'll

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:08.510
even get a free tier,
where you can experiment

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:12.340
with the different services and
see if they meet your needs.

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:14.970
And you can follow
this graph here kind of

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:17.950
is a very basic model
of how Cloud costs can

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:22.030
compare to very strictly
managed on premise costs.

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:25.030
So as demand rises for
on premise servers,

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:29.302
the engineers will have new
servers spin up over time.

00:13:29.302 --> 00:13:30.760
And then once demand
drops, they'll

